      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n-----\n\nThe Google C++ Style Guide mandates that comments should explain the *why* (intent, subtle edge cases, or workarounds), never the *what* or *how* (which should be obvious from well-named variables and standard C++ constructs). It also explicitly bans \"ascii-art\" and decorative banners.\n\nHere is a comprehensive breakdown of all the instances in your codebase where comments violate these principles and should be removed.\n\n### 1. File Inclusion Headers\n\nAcross almost every `.cc` and `.h` file, you have categorized your `#include` directives:\n\n```cpp\n// C system files\n// C++ system files\n// Other libraries\u0027 headers\n// Project headers\n\n```\n\n**Why remove:** The Google Style Guide mandates a specific ordering for includes (matching header, C library, C++ library, other libraries, project headers). Because the order is strictly defined by the style guide (and enforced by `clang-format`), labeling the sections is considered redundant noise.\n\n### 2. Decorative Banner Comments\n\nGoogle Style explicitly forbids using lines of dashes or equals signs to create visual sections in code. The structure of the code (namespaces, classes, blank lines) should provide the visual breaks.\n\n**Remove these from `bootrom.cc`, `bootrom.h`, `bootrom_test.cc`, `ec_comm_test.cc`, `flasher_logic_test.cc`, `fake_usb_comm.h`:**\n\n* `// \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d`\n* `// Bootrom Communication Implementation`\n* `// --- Fake-Based Tests ---`\n* `// --- Basic Bootrom commands ---`\n* `// --- Security related ---`\n* `// --- Memory / Flash operations (Secure Channel) ---`\n* `// ---------------------------------------------------------`\n* `// PHASE 1: Encrypt` (and phases 2 \u0026 3)\n* `// --- UsbInterface implementation ---`\n* `// --- Test helpers ---`\n\n### 3. Redundant Struct Field Comments\n\nIn `util/egis/ec_protocol_defs.h`, the inline comments just repeat what the C++ types and variable names already say:\n\n```cpp\nstruct EcHostCommandRequest {\n  uint8_t protocol_version \u003d 3;  // Protocol version (typically 3) -\u003e REMOVE\n  uint8_t checksum \u003d 0;          // Packet checksum -\u003e REMOVE\n  uint16_t command_id;           // Command ID (little-endian) -\u003e REMOVE\n  uint8_t command_version;       // Command version -\u003e REMOVE\n  uint8_t reserved \u003d 0;          // Reserved / padding -\u003e REMOVE\n  uint16_t data_length;          // Payload length (little-endian) -\u003e REMOVE\n} __attribute__((packed));\n\n```\n\n**Why remove:** `uint8_t checksum` is perfectly self-descriptive. Only leave comments on fields if there are unexpected constraints (e.g., bitwise requirements or unusual default states).\n\n### 4. \"Play-by-Play\" Narration Comments\n\nAvoid commenting every step of a function as if narrating a script. The code itself tells the story.\n\n**In `util/egis/et171_flash.cc`:**\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Parse Arguments\n// 2. Initialize USB Context\n// 3. Connect to Device\n// Print diagnostic information from the device\n// 4. Dispatch to Flasher\n\n```\n\n**In `util/egis/ec_comm.cc`:**\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Pack the header and payload instantly without memcpy\n// 2. Apply the checksum\n\n```\n\n**In `util/egis/bootrom.cc`:**\n\n```cpp\n// Return a safe subspan covering everything after the header\nreturn raw_response.subspan(...);\n\n// Normalize endianness before returning to the application layer\ninfo.version \u003d FromLittleEndian(info.version);\n\n```\n\n**In `util/egis/cli_options.cc`:**\n\n```cpp\n// Strip standard hex prefixes\nif (strv.starts_with(\"0x\") || strv.starts_with(\"0X\")) { ... }\n\n// Catch overflow or formatting errors natively reported by from_chars\nif (ec !\u003d std::errc{}) { ... }\n\n```\n\n**In `util/egis/bootrom.h`:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, BootromError\u003e DisableWp();  // Disable SPI write-protect\n\n```\n\n*(The function is named `DisableWp`—the comment adds zero new information.)*\n\n**In `util/egis/usb_comm.h`:**\n\n```cpp\nclass UsbComm : public UsbInterface {\n public:  // Require the caller to provide the context\n  UsbComm(libusb_context *ctx...);\n\n```\n\n### 5. Over-Commented Test Suites\n\nTest suites are often the worst offenders for \"play-by-play\" comments. Google Style prefers that the Arrange/Act/Assert blocks of a test speak for themselves.\n\n**Remove these from `util/egis/bootrom_test.cc`:**\n\n* `// We can verify that the host actually sent exactly one command packet` (preceding `EXPECT_EQ(..., 1)`)\n* `// Push a corrupted response` (preceding `/*corrupt_tag\u003d*/true`)\n* `// Verify one command was sent.` (preceding `EXPECT_EQ(..., 1)`)\n* `// Verify the host properly chunked the requests into two sends` (preceding `EXPECT_EQ(..., 2)`)\n\n**Remove these from `util/egis/ec_comm_test.cc`:**\n\n* `// Calculated below` (next to `checksum \u003d 0;`)\n* `// Push a successful response with no payload`\n* `// Verify the host properly dispatched the command packet`\n* `// Recalculate checksum after modification.`\n* `// A response with only 4 bytes is too short for a full header.`\n* `// Construct a header that claims a 64-byte payload is attached`\n* `// Manually overwrite the data_length field to an invalid value`\n* `// The parser must catch the size mismatch and abort safely`\n* `// Simulate the EC returning kAccessDenied (0x0004)`\n\n**Remove these from `util/egis/cli_options_test.cc`:**\n\n* `// Invalid algorithm should fail parsing entirely` (preceding `EXPECT_FALSE(...)`)\n* `// Valid hex values`\n* `// Invalid hex value should fail`\n* `// Empty invocation fails`\n* `// Missing the value for --offset entirely`\n\n**Remove these from `util/egis/crypto_util_test.cc`:**\n\n* `// 1. Encrypt`, `// 2. Decrypt`, `// 3. Verify`\n* `// Encrypt with empty AAD` (preceding `{}`)\n* `// Corrupt the tag` (preceding `tag[0] ^\u003d 0xFF`)\n* `// Corrupt the ciphertext`\n* `// Use incorrect AAD during decryption`\n\n**Remove these from `util/egis/file_util_test.cc`:**\n\n* `// Helper to quickly generate binary files for testing`\n* `// Generate exactly 32 bytes of test key data`\n* `// Verify the data was successfully copied into the fixed-size array`\n\n### What to KEEP:\n\nYou have some excellent comments in this codebase that *should* remain because they explain the *why*:\n\n* `// CRITICAL: Perform this check *before* subtracting... to prevent a potential integer underflow...`\n* `// Catch trailing garbage (e.g., \"1000xyz\")` (Explains edge-cases).\n* `// Reset getopt\u0027s internal state for re-entrant parsing in tests.` (Explains a workaround for a C library quirk).\n* `// It is cryptographically improbable (1 in 2^96) for two generated IVs to match...` (Explains the mathematical theory behind the test expectation).",
