      "message": "Detailed instructions from Gemini:\n\n----\n\n### Step 1: Add the Helper to `ec_protocol_defs.h`\n\nSince both the host request and the device response share the exact same checksum algorithm and memory layout (the checksum is always at byte offset 1), we can put a generic helper directly in the protocol definitions file.\n\n**Add this to the bottom of `util/egis/ec_protocol_defs.h` (inside `namespace egis`):**\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003ccstddef\u003e\n#include \u003cspan\u003e\n\nnamespace egis {\n\n// ... (existing structs and enums) ...\n\n// Ensure requests and responses have the checksum at the exact same byte offset\nstatic_assert(offsetof(EcHostCommandRequest, checksum) \u003d\u003d \n              offsetof(EcHostCommandResponse, checksum),\n              \"Checksum offset must be identical for requests and responses\");\n\n// Computes and applies the two\u0027s-complement checksum required by the EC protocol.\ninline void ApplyEcChecksum(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e packet) {\n  constexpr size_t kChecksumOffset \u003d offsetof(EcHostCommandRequest, checksum);\n  if (packet.size() \u003c\u003d kChecksumOffset) return;\n\n  // 1. Clear the existing checksum byte\n  packet[kChecksumOffset] \u003d 0;\n\n  // 2. Sum all bytes\n  uint32_t sum \u003d 0;\n  for (uint8_t byte : packet) {\n    sum +\u003d byte;\n  }\n\n  // 3. Apply the two\u0027s complement\n  packet[kChecksumOffset] \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n}\n\n}  // namespace egis\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Simplify `ec_comm.cc`\n\nNow we remove the private `UpdateChecksum` method from the `EcCommand` wrapper and replace it with our new helper.\n\n**Modify `util/egis/ec_comm.cc`:**\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Remove the private UpdateChecksum() method from the class entirely:\nclass EcCommand {\n public:\n  EcCommand(uint16_t cmd_id, uint8_t cmd_ver,\n            std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload \u003d {}) {\n    // ... [validation and header creation unchanged] ...\n\n    // 1. Pack the header and payload instantly without memcpy\n    cmd_bytes_ \u003d PackMessage(hdr, payload);\n\n    // 2. Apply the checksum\n    ApplyEcChecksum(cmd_bytes_);\n  }\n\n  const std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e \u0026bytes() const { return cmd_bytes_; }\n\n private:\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd_bytes_;\n  \n  // REMOVED: void UpdateChecksum() { ... }\n};\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: DRY out the Test Suite (`ec_comm_test.cc`)\n\nYour test suite was manually recalculating the checksum in four different places. Now it becomes clean and expressive.\n\n**1. Simplify `MakeMockResponse`:**\n\n```cpp\n  // Helper to construct mathematically sound EC responses with valid checksums\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e MakeMockResponse(uint16_t result_code,\n                                        std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload \u003d {}) {\n    EcHostCommandResponse hdr{\n        .protocol_version \u003d 3,\n        .checksum \u003d 0,\n        .result \u003d ToLittleEndian(result_code),\n        .data_length \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint16_t\u003e(payload.size()))};\n\n    auto packet \u003d PackMessage(hdr, payload);\n    \n    // Clean, single-line checksum application\n    ApplyEcChecksum(packet);\n    return packet;\n  }\n\n```\n\n**2. Simplify `InvalidProtocolVersionIsRejected`:**\n\n```cpp\nTEST_F(EcCommTest, InvalidProtocolVersionIsRejected) {\n  auto packet \u003d MakeMockResponse(0);\n\n  // The EC protocol dictates version 3.\n  packet[offsetof(EcHostCommandResponse, protocol_version)] \u003d 4;\n\n  // REPLACE the 5 lines of manual recalculation with:\n  ApplyEcChecksum(packet);\n\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(packet);\n  // ...\n\n```\n\n**3. Simplify `TruncatedPayloadIsCaught`:**\n\n```cpp\nTEST_F(EcCommTest, TruncatedPayloadIsCaught) {\n  // ... [setup unchanged] ...\n\n  // Manually overwrite the data_length field to an invalid value\n  *reinterpret_cast\u003cuint16_t*\u003e(\n      \u0026packet[offsetof(EcHostCommandResponse, data_length)]) \u003d\n      ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint16_t\u003e(64));\n\n  // REPLACE the 5 lines of manual recalculation with:\n  ApplyEcChecksum(packet);\n\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(packet);\n  // ...\n\n```\n\n### Result\n\nBy centralizing this math, you eliminate over 20 lines of repetitive boilerplate. If you ever need to debug the checksum logic or alter how it iterates (for performance or diagnostic reasons), you now only have to change it in one place, and the entire test suite and host tool instantly inherit the fix.",
